/-!
# Elementary structure of Goldschmidt triples

This family proves the following source assertions for finite groups with a
genuine `IsGoldschmidtTriple`. All local cores and residuals are embedded
through their actual subtype maps.

* F.6.5(1): `quotient_twoCore`, `quotient_twoCore_eq_bot`, and
  `quotient_twoCore_intersection` transport the generators and their overlap.
  `quotientTwoCoreAmalgamIso` identifies all three intrinsic quotients with
  their images and preserves both inclusion squares.
* F.6.6: `twoResidual_eq_sup` identifies the residual generated by the two
  local residuals. The normalization and supplement lemmas also give the
  literal product in `twoResidual_mul_intersection`.
* F.6.7: `source_lemma_F_6_7` proves all three equivalent conditions for
  either generator and an actually subnormal subgroup, with a Sylow witness
  inside that subgroup.
* F.6.8: `normal_quotient_structure` gives the containment, odd order and
  solvability of the literal quotient `XQ/Q`. Solvability uses the
  unconditional Odd Order Theorem.
* F.6.11: `primeToThreeCore_structure` supplies the Sylow witness and
  solvability of the prime-to-three core. `primeToThreeCore_dichotomy` gives
  both quotient alternatives, including the compatible amalgam isomorphism
  and noncyclicity in the collapsed case.
* F.6.12: `left_invariant_subgroup_eq_bot` and
  `right_invariant_subgroup_eq_bot` use actual Sylow three-subgroups and
  normality relative to the join of the local core and opposite residual.
  The corresponding characteristic-subgroup theorems give part (2).
* F.6.14: `odd_residual_small_sylow` retains both `O₂(G) = 1` and
  `O²(G) = O(G)`, and constructs the cyclic or Klein-four isomorphisms,
  including both local cores in the latter alternative.

The prime-to-three-core obstruction uses the unique normal cubic residual
of a symmetric-three supplement to a cyclic normal subgroup. The rigidity
argument uses the Frattini argument and the common-normal-core theorem;
the small Sylow alternatives use the two quotient maps with trivial common
kernel. These proofs need no amalgam enumeration or additional recognition
hypotheses. The general classification assertions F.6.5(2)–(3), F.6.9–10,
F.6.13 and F.6.15–18 belong to the other section modules.

Source: Aschbacher–Smith, *The Classification of Quasithin Groups*,
§F.6, printed pp.304–307 (1 March 2004 manuscript).
-/
